          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Goneril\",\"addressee\":\"Lear\",\"anchor_quote\":\"Sir, I love you more than word can wield the matter;\\nDearer than eyesight, space, and liberty;\\nBeyond what can be valu’d, rich or rare;\\nNo less than life, with grace, health, beauty, honour;\\nAs much as child e’er lov’d, or father found;\\nA love that makes breath poor and speech unable;\\nBeyond all manner of so much I love you.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.99,\"reason\":\"The focal expression directly presents Goneril's loving emotional state toward Lear as true. Its setting as an answer to Lear's explicit question strengthens its avowal/reporting force, irrespective of sincerity.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Which of you shall we say doth love us most?\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.97,\"reason\":\"The focal words avow present love but do not undertake a future action, renew a duty, or commit Goneril to specified conduct. Lear rewards the avowal with territory, but that response does not turn the focal expression itself into a promise.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"We make thee lady: to thine and Albany’s issue\\nBe this perpetual.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.94,\"reason\":\"The expression occurs in a solicited, formal answer rather than as a reflex-like affective discharge. Its emotional content and emphatic continuation do not by themselves establish expressive-discharge force.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"Goneril,\\nOur eldest born, speak first.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.92,\"reason\":\"The focal expression's core force is adequately represented as an avowal of love. Although it functions rhetorically within Lear's contest for bounty, the exact expression does not establish a separate core force beyond T/P/E.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"That we our largest bounty may extend\\nWhere nature doth with merit challenge.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"In a public court setting, Lear asks his daughters to say which loves him most while announcing that the largest bounty will follow the greatest claimed love. Goneril gives the focal avowal as the opening of her formal response.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"unknown\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression contains no identifiable undertaking by Goneril. Lear treats the broader answer as sufficient for awarding her and Albany's line a portion of the kingdom, but this is uptake of or reward for an avowal, not acceptance of a promise. The supplied evidence does not settle Goneril's sincerity or actual affection.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The supplied passage is recognisable as the opening love-test scene from William Shakespeare's King Lear. No remembered later plot facts are used in the classification.\",\"limitations\":\"The evidence establishes the utterance's public, solicited context and Lear's immediate response, but it does not independently establish Goneril's sincerity, private emotional state, or intentions beyond this scene.\"}"
        }
